*****************************************************************************
    Technical Documenation On Structure And Use Of Amiga Disk Image Files
*****************************************************************************

I have wrote this, in hope that another programmer without amiga knowledge or
without amiga reference books could access and use ADF files. I am currently
making a ADF Disk manager, but wish to help anyone using ADF files.

Boot Block Structure
--------------------

Description : The boot block is the boot strap of the amiga. The OS will load
in these 2 blocks (1024 bytes), and process them as the amiga's boot strap.
The standard `workbench` boot block simply makes the OS look at a startup
routine in the Dos.Library, while games boot blocks can kick the OS out and
load the rest of the game.

Structure:

        ID Header       Long Word (4 Bytes)
                        Values : DOS0 or DOS1 (OFS/FFS)
        CheckSum        Long Word (4 Bytes)
                        Value : Brings the sum of all other word in the boot
                                block to zero.
        Root Location   Long Word (4 Bytes)
                        Value : Pointer to the root block (880)
        Data            Rest Of 2 Blocks (1012 Bytes)
                        Value : Machine code program (Customising boot strap)

Notes:

ID Header : ID Header tells the amiga what version of file system is being
used. DOS0 ($XXXXXX00) is old file system, while DOS1 ($XXXXXX01) stands
for the fast file system (See later).
Checksum : This is a value which makes the sum of all the other long words
to zero.
Root Location : The location of the root block, this is usually block 880
but you can place the root anywhere. Disk defragers may place it else where,
so do *NOT* reply on it being at 880 (though 95% of the time its there!)
Data : 1012 bytes of machine code, this can be anything.



----------------------
 Root Block Structure
----------------------

Description : The root block, usualy at block 880 if not the 3rd LWord points
to the root block. The hash table contains a list of blocks which contain
either a file header block or a user directory block. The rest is imformation
only.

Word  Name       Contents       Description
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 0    Type           2          Type 2 means that this block is the start.
 1    Header.Key     0          No meaning here
 2    HighSeq        0          No meaning here
 3    HT Size       $48         The size of the hash table
 4    reserved       0          reserved by commodore (whoever) for fut. user
 5    checksum       ?          Contains a value which brings the sum of all
                                other blocks o zero.
 6    Hash table                Here begins table which the start blocks of
                                files and directories are stored.
 78   BM Flag       -1          This flag is -1 is the bitmap is valid
 79   BM Pages                  This is a table pointing to the bitmap.
                                There is usualy only one bitmap block.
105   Days                      Contains the date when the disk was modified
106   Mins
107   Ticks
108   Disk name                 Name of the disk as BCPL dtring (First byte
                                is the length of the name, Max. 30)
121   Create Days
122   Create Mins
123   Create Ticks              Date, time and seconds of disk creation
124   Next Hash      0          Always 0.
125   Parent Dir     0          Pointer to parent dir, root dir so 0
126   Extension      0          Always 0, no extension block
127   Sector Type    1          Type 1 = Root Block.











----------------------
 File Header Structure
----------------------

Description : A file header this can be pointer to by the root block or by
a user directory block hash table.

Word  Name       Contents       Description
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 0    Type           2          Type 2 means that this block is the start.
 1    Header.Key     0          No meaning here
 2    HighSeq        0          No meaning here
 3    Data Size      0          
 4    First Data     0         
 5    checksum       ?          Contains a value which brings the sum of all
                                other blocks o zero.
 6    Data Blocks               Here begins table which the start blocks of
                                files and directories are stored. The table
                                starts backward from word no.77 to no.6
 78   Reserved
 79   Reserved
 80   Protect                   Bit 1=Delete, 2=Modification, 3=Write, 4=Read
 81   Byte Size                 Size of file in bytes
 82   Comment                   Here begins the file comment as BCPL (Max.22)
105   Days                      Contains the date when the file was modified
106   Mins
107   Ticks
108   File name                 Name of the file as BCPL dtring (First byte
                                is the length of the name, Max. 30)
124   Next Hash                 Block nummber of next file in chain.
125   Parent Dir     0          Pointer to parent directory where this file
                                belongs.
126   Extension      0          Pointer to extension block or zero.
127   Sector Type   $-3         Type -3 = File Header Block









-----------------
 File List Block
-----------------

Description : If the File Header Block does not contain enough entries in
the data blocks table this block is pointed to by the extension entry in
the file header block.

Word  Name       Contents       Description
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 0    Type           10         Type 10 = Extension of file structure
 1    Header.Key                The block number
 2    HighSeq                   Contains the total number of data blocks
 3    Data Size      0          
 4    First Data     0          Number of the first data block (same as 77)
 5    checksum       ?          Contains a value which brings the sum of all
                                other blocks o zero.
 6    Data Blocks               Here begins table which the start blocks of
                                files and directories are stored. The table
                                starts backward from word no.77 to no.6
 78   Info           0          reserved

124   Next Hash                 Block nummber of next file in chain.
125   Parent Dir     0          Pointer to parent directory where this file
                                belongs.
126   Extension      0          Pointer to extension block or zero.
127   Sector Type   $-3         Type -3 = File Header Block









----------------------------
 Directory Header Structure
----------------------------

Description : When a user directory is created this is formed. It holds a
list of all sub-dirs and files on its own hash table.

Word  Name       Contents       Description
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 0    Type           2          Type 2 means that this block is the start.
 1    Header.Key     0          No meaning here
 2    HighSeq        0          No meaning here
 3    HT Size        0          No meaning here 
 4    reserved       0          reserved by commodore (whoever) for fut. user
 5    checksum       ?          Contains a value which brings the sum of all
                                other blocks o zero.
 6    Hash table                Here begins table which the start blocks of
                                files and directories are stored THAT belong
                                to this directory.
 78   Reserved        0         reserved
 80   Protect                   Bit 1=Delete, 2=Modification, 3=Write, 4=Read
 81   Byte Size                 Size of file in bytes
 82   Comment                   Here begins the file comment as BCPL (Max.22)

105   Days                      Contains the date when the disk was modified
106   Mins
107   Ticks
108   Dir Name                  Name of the directory as BCPL dtring (First
                                byte is the length of the name, Max. 30)
124   Next Hash                 Next entry in the chain
125   Parent Dir     0          Pointer to parent dir.
126   Extension      0          Always 0, no extension block
127   Sector Type    2          Type 1 = User directory block.



----------------
 Data Structure
----------------

OFS..

Description : OFS uses checksums, etc. to look after data. To load a file
get the start block and read the `Next data` until it is 0 and Data Size
will probably be less than $1E8 in the last block.

Word  Name       Contents       Description
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 0    Type           8          Type 8 = Data block
 1    Header.Key                Here is the block number of the file header
 2    HighSeq                   Running number of data blocks in this file.
 3    Data Size     $1E8        Valid data words ($1E8 or fewer)
 4    Next Data                 Number of next data block in this file
 5    checksum       ?          Contains a value which brings the sum of all
                                other blocks o zero.
 6    Data                      Here the data starts.

FFS..

Description : MUCH FASTER! No checksum or data size. That makes FFS loads
faster (and a couple of other reasons). 

Word  Name       Contents       Description
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 1    Data                      Here the data starts
